---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('drip')!;
const b = appBySlug('postscript')!;
const path = '/compare/drip-vs-postscript';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/drip-vs-postscript');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/drip-vs-postscript');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "What is this comparison deciding?",
    "answer": "Whether the demonstrated workflow benefit repays the change in audience, channel and operating costs."
  },
  {
    "question": "Are these measured recovery results?",
    "answer": "No. The workflows are proposed acceptance exercises and the basket calculations are hypothetical gross-profit examples."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can both systems send for the same cart?",
    "answer": "Only with explicit ownership and demonstrated exclusions. During evaluation, assign each eligible cart to one recovery owner per channel."
  },
  {
    "question": "What must survive migration?",
    "answer": "Permission provenance, opt-outs, pending-cart treatment and a ledger of messages already sent must survive the change."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
<a href="/compare" class="link">← Comparisons</a>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
<p class="mt-7 text-xl">Measure what an additional SMS branch earns alongside an existing Drip email program.</p>
<TldrBox picks={[{"label": "Evaluate Drip", "tool": "Drip", "reason": "Prove the required cart workflow and account access."}, {"label": "Evaluate Postscript", "tool": "Postscript", "reason": "Compare the validated operating benefit with staying in the current setup."}]} />
<article class="prose-shopify mt-12 max-w-4xl">
<h2>Evaluate the marginal SMS branch</h2>
<p>For a store already sending Drip email, the useful question is whether Postscript adds profitable reach among SMS subscribers who still have an open checkout. This is a channel-budget decision before it is a replacement decision. Compare Drip email alone, an eligible Drip SMS branch and a Postscript SMS branch only where the offered accounts can support them. Do not assign both tools the same pending text.</p>
<h2>Confirm the account scope first</h2>
<p><a href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/48924885546125-How-to-Activate-SMS">Drip’s SMS activation guide</a> currently limits access to US customers with Support All People enabled. Review, approval and sending-number registration precede activation. An email account alone does not establish SMS availability. <a href="https://postscript.io/features">Postscript’s feature documentation</a> describes SMS collection at supported checkouts and event-based flows, including checkout-started and order-created events. Confirm recipient markets, access and quoted usage for the actual account. Sources checked November 25, 2026.</p>
<h2>Separate cart progress from checkout abandonment</h2>
<p>Postscript’s <a href="https://help.postscript.io/en/articles/13564025-understanding-infinity-testing-in-automation-flows">automation testing guide</a> specifies cancellation at Checkout Started or Order Created for a Product Added to Cart trigger. That distinction matters: a shopper advancing to checkout should not keep receiving the earlier cart nudge. Demonstrate the cancellation in the selected flow, including when events arrive out of order.</p>
<p>On the Drip side, its <a href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/42835644176397-Shopify-Updated-a-Cart-vs-Checkout-Updated-Created-Events">cart-event guide</a> distinguishes identified cart activity from checkout events. Products added after an email is queued will not be included in it. Inspect what the waiting email says before pairing it with a text that may refer to a newer checkout state.</p>
<h2>Proposed exercise: challenge the second channel</h2>
<p>Begin with a customer eligible for both email and SMS. Start a cart, advance to checkout, and buy while a text is waiting. Record event arrival, cancellation and send timestamps in both systems. Repeat with SMS-only permission, email-only permission, a phone number without SMS permission, and a subscriber who opts out during the wait. A phone field is not proof that a marketing text should send.</p>
<p>Then trigger a welcome offer and cart recovery together. Assign one owner to the discount and a store-specific cross-channel contact budget. If quiet hours defer a text, recheck purchase, permission and cart validity when it becomes eligible again. A delayed reminder should not arrive after the order confirmation. These are acceptance exercises, not observed success rates or promised safeguards.</p>
<h2>Give SMS a different job from email</h2>
<p>Email can explain fit, delivery and bundle terms. A text should provide a concise useful next action for an unresolved eligible checkout, not repeat the same paragraphs. For international shipping shock, use the current quote; do not imply a coupon covers duties. For a size that sold out, stop the invalid checkout prompt. Gift instructions and a mistaken subscription choice need assistance rather than urgency.</p>
<p>For financing or BNPL friction, link to accurate payment guidance without promising approval. A bundle discount or wholesale MOQ block belongs with merchandising or support before another reminder is scheduled. Repeat buyers with saved addresses may need only a return link; first-time mobile shoppers may need reassurance. Measure the work of maintaining these branches across two systems when deciding whether the extra channel is worth retaining.</p>
<h2>Charge the SMS branch for discounts as well as sends</h2>
<p>Hypothetical fixed-goods-cost economics: a $202 cart at 36% margin has $129.28 goods cost and $72.72 gross profit. A 10% code leaves $181.80 revenue and $52.52 gross profit, reducing gross profit by $20.20. A $392 cart at 54% margin has $180.32 goods cost and $211.68 gross profit. An illustrative 20% code leaves $313.60 revenue and $133.28 gross profit, reducing it by $78.40. These are scenarios, not recommended discount limits; shipping, payment costs, returns, tax and messaging fees remain to be deducted.</p>
<p>Obtain the offered base fees, message billing units, carrier charges, recipient-market rates, minimums and support costs. Add the labor to reconcile Drip and Postscript exclusions. Allocate shared subscription costs consistently, then ask how much additional contribution the SMS branch needs to cover its incremental expense. Gross platform-attributed revenue cannot answer that question.</p>
<h2>Measure incremental orders among eligible subscribers</h2>
<p>Keep Drip email unchanged for comparable SMS-eligible customers. Randomly withhold the additional SMS branch from a suitable comparison cohort, with a documented assignment and observation window. Reconcile Shopify order IDs once, deduct incentive and variable costs, and compare opt-outs as well as orders. Do not add both vendors’ attributed revenue for the same checkout or assume every SMS-click order was caused by the text. Report uncertainty if order counts are small.</p>
<h2>Move the SMS owner without moving the whole store</h2>
<p>Inventory active SMS campaigns, welcome flows, cart flows, phone consent evidence and opt-out records. Validate import status and number setup with the destination provider; retain Drip email if it is staying. Decide how pending texts will finish before routing new checkout events to the replacement. Freeze overlapping campaigns during the switch so an automation test is not confused with a broadcast.</p>
<p>Start with a limited eligible cohort and inspect sends, exits and complaints. If cancellation or consent reconciliation fails, pause the new text queue while keeping the validated email path available. Restore the old SMS owner only after excluding carts already contacted by the new one. Keep Postscript when incremental contribution and the operational benefit justify a separate SMS owner; keep the simpler setup when the added branch does not repay its cost.</p>
</article>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} title={`${a.name} vs ${b.name} FAQ`} />
</div></section>
</BaseLayout>
